 "HOW_A_READER_IS_SUPPOSED_TO_KNOW": "By inferring it from the KEY NAME. There is no marker in the data. A field is live if its key does not look like one of the suffixes above -- a judgement the reader must make from an unwritten and growing convention.",
 "IS_THE_CONVENTION_DOCUMENTED": {
  "deposits_documenting_it": [],
  "finding": "NONE. No deposit defines which suffixes mean not-live. The convention exists only in the practice of the rounds that created it."
 },
 "WHY_AN_EXCLUSION_LIST_CANNOT_WORK": "An enumerated suffix list is exactly as complete as its author's memory, and the vocabulary GROWS every repair round -- ORIGINAL_REFUTED and PREMISE_REFUTED were created at r341 and broke a filter written before them. Any checker built by enumeration is stale on arrival, which is the same perishability defect as an undated mtime, one level up: the CHECK is perishable, not just the fact.",
 "PROPOSAL_NOT_IMPLEMENTED": {
  "form": "A single explicit marker on the field's own container, e.g. {\"value\": ..., \"live\": false, \"superseded_by\": \"<key>\", \"as_of\": \"<ts>\"}. Subordination becomes a PROPERTY OF THE DATA rather than of the key name.",
  "what_it_buys": "A checker becomes 'live is not false' -- one rule, no vocabulary, immune to new suffixes. Any reader, human or agent, applies it without prior knowledge.",
